From: andreas.ehrencrona@p1.noja.ct.se (Andreas Ehrencrona)
Date: Thu, 28 Apr 94 21:59:00 +0200
Subject: [#94-4-271] SCI:Artificial Intelligence

 BR-P> Perhaps passing the Turing test would be the point at which an AI should
 BR-P> be given full rights and citizenship.  This might be a sticky problem if
 BR-P> the hardware it is being run on is expensive, but if we can't tell it
 BR-P> from a human, how can we say that turning it off isn't murder?

We've been discussing this a lot on FidoNet here in Sweden, but no consensus
seems to arise. Personally I think that we might somewhere far in the future
see a community of AIs that have citizenship (possibly national, possibly
forming their own nation) that own their own hardware and from whom services
are bought. It would certainly solve a lot of these kind of problems giving AIs
autonomy.

Also, the problem of how in a networked system to discern "individuals", would
be solved if we treated all AIs as a nation/person.

Personally I find this vision appealing, but probably the researcher who
invents the first AI won't. Also, this is a solution for the long-term. The
first-generation AIs would be research projects that would eventually have to
be scrapped in favor of other experiments.

 BR-P> What rights should a Turing-passing AI have?  Is the right to life,
 BR-P> liberty, etc merely the right to backup, or to be run?  If the AI starts
 BR-P> cloning itself (reproduction?) does each copy have the right to live?
 BR-P> How different does the child process have to be to be considered a
 BR-P> unique entity?

 BR-P> Brian

All AIs that are comparable to a human in intelligence, should have the right
to live, regardless of whether it is humanlike or not. But then again, AIs have
not evolved, and therefore might not have the *will* to live, or could even be
programmed to commit "suicide" after a certain time, and to "want" to do it
(I'm not saying this is not connected to a lot of moral problems).

   andreas e


